          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "why": "iterative is the smallest-area family (single shared datapath, no per-stage registers). At 100 MHz it needs <=10 cycles/result to hit 1 MSPS, so n_iter 10-14 with data_width 12-16 covers the accuracy/area trade-off: W=12/N=10 is the minimal feasible corner, while W=14-16/N=12-14 buys accuracy_bits toward the HV reference of 10 at modest area cost. angle_guard -2..2 and frac_guard 0..2 sweep the accuracy levers that are free or cheap in area."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 16
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 10,
          "high": 14
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": -2,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "why": "unrolled_k raises throughput per cycle (k rotations/cycle) so it can meet 1 MSPS at a lower Fmax or with fewer n_iter, which can trade off against area. k=2-4 with n_iter 10-14 keeps latency short and lets the search find whether unrolling beats iterative on area at equal accuracy. Same data_width/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding ranges as iterative so the two fronts are directly comparable and merge cleanly."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "No fleet facts yet, so I seed both allowed families. The spec is area-dominated with a hard throughput floor (>=1 MSPS) and a hard accuracy floor (max_abs_err <= 2^-10, i.e. accuracy_bits >= 10). iterative is the natural area-minimiser (shared datapath, no pipeline registers) but is throughput-limited by its N+3 cycle latency, so I cap n_iter at 14 to stay near the 10-cycle budget at 100 MHz. unrolled_k is included because k>1 can hit 1 MSPS with fewer iterations or a lower clock, potentially at lower area than a longer iterative chain; k is kept to 2-4 to avoid the area blow-up of wide unrolled datapaths. data_width 12-16 brackets the minimal W that can reach accuracy_bits >= 10 up to a comfortable margin toward the HV reference, and angle_guard/frac_guard sweep the cheap accuracy levers. Rounding is left to both choices since it shifts accuracy at negligible area cost. The 55/45 split favours iterative (the likely area winner) while still mapping the unrolled trade-off so the merged front covers the curve."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established and robust. The minimum area design (iterative, 6987 um^2) comfortably satisfies the throughput constraint (9.43 MSPS >= 1) and the accuracy constraint (10.2 bits >= 10). The front extends to higher accuracy (11.9 bits) at the cost of area, providing a good trade-off curve. Since this is the final round, we stop and select the minimum area design from the front as per the spec's selection rule."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
